% One page placing one form many times, painted twice: directly by a
% device that paints, and through a device that writes down what it was
% asked to paint and plays it back into one. run-form-band-test.sh runs
% this file once per route, compares the pages it leaves, and reads the
% numbers printed below.
%
% A form is a self-contained description painted at several locations,
% and the language allows an interpreter to keep the graphical output of
% one and substitute it for a later execution rather than running the
% description again (PLRM 4.7). This interpreter does that where the
% device can be stood in for by a recorder, which is a device declaring
% the five marking methods, a colour space a record holds, no path
% filling and no screen.
%
% What is kept is the marks the description made, held by a recorder
% standing in for the device, and every use after the first places that
% drawing where it is wanted. It is kept on both routes: a device that
% writes down what it is asked to paint holds no rows, and a drawing is
% not rows.
%
% The page below is built so that the difference has somewhere to show:
% one form, placed many times, at a placement count the run varies, so
% that how the count of executions moves with the count of placements is
% a reading rather than a single number.
%
% What is printed, and why each is needed:
%
%   PAINTS      how many times the form's description was executed. It
%               is the whole quantity: a page comparison cannot see it,
%               since a form re-executed paints exactly what a form
%               copied paints.
%   PLACEMENTS  how many times the page used the form. PAINTS is read
%               against this and not on its own -- a cache that works
%               holds PAINTS still while this grows.
%
% The run varies three things through -D, so that one page serves every
% reading:
%
%   N     placements. Two values, so the cache claim is about how the
%         count moves rather than about one number.
%   FRAC  place the form at fractional device offsets rather than whole
%         ones, and at two different fractions, so that a drawing held
%         is placed at points between pixels it was not made at. A
%         drawing is the marks the description made and a placement
%         carries them, so the page must be the page painted afresh
%         wherever the placements fall.
%   AFRESH  put the page under a clip no drawing can be held under,
%         which takes the copy out of the picture on a device that would
%         otherwise make one. It is how the run reaches a direct
%         rendering on the route that caches, and it is the control for
%         the cache reading: the same page, the same route, the same
%         description under the same transformation, the copy the only
%         thing withdrawn.

/N where { pop }{ /N 25 def } ifelse
/FRAC where { pop }{ /FRAC 0 def } ifelse
/AFRESH where { pop }{ /AFRESH 0 def } ifelse

/W 400 def
/H 400 def
<< /PageSize [W H] >> setpagedevice

globaldict /paints 0 put

/F <<
    /FormType 1
    /BBox [0 0 60 60]
    /Matrix [1 0 0 1 0 0]
    /PaintProc {
        pop
        globaldict /paints globaldict /paints get 1 add put
        0.2 setgray 0 0 60 60 rectfill
        0.9 setgray 10 10 40 40 rectfill
        0 setgray 5 5 moveto 55 55 lineto 3 setlinewidth stroke
    }
>> def

% Withdraw the copy, and nothing else. A drawing is held only under a
% clip that is an axis-aligned rectangle in the device's own coordinates
% -- what reaches a recorder is already cut to the clip, so a clip of any
% other shape is one a captured drawing could not be placed under. So a
% square turned forty-five degrees and large enough to hold the whole
% page withdraws the copy while cutting nothing: every placement runs
% the description where it stands, and the page is the page.
%
% Sealing the dictionary is not this control and cannot be: execform
% stamps its own entry into the operand dictionary and makes it
% read-only, and PLRM 8.2 has that succeed whether or not the program
% got there first.
AFRESH 0 ne {
    newpath
    W 2 div H 2 div 900 sub moveto
    W 2 div 900 add H 2 div lineto
    W 2 div H 2 div 900 add lineto
    W 2 div 900 sub H 2 div lineto
    closepath clip newpath
} if

% The placements: a square grid holding N of them, spread over the page.
% The step is a whole number of device units, or a half short of one
% where the run asked for fractional placement.
/cols N sqrt ceiling cvi def
/gaps cols 1 sub def
gaps 1 lt { /gaps 1 def } if
/step W 70 sub gaps div truncate def
% A half unit on the step, not on the origin: what a copied form is
% quantized against is where the placement falls between pixels, and a
% whole-numbered step puts every placement at the same such phase --
% including the one the copy was taken at, which is then right for all
% of them. Half a unit on the step gives the placements two phases, so
% a copy taken at one of them is standing half a pixel off at the other.
FRAC 0 ne { /step step 0.5 add def } if

0 1 N 1 sub {
    /i exch def
    gsave
        i cols mod step mul 5 add
        i cols idiv step mul 5 add
        translate
        F execform
    grestore
} for

showpage

% a line of its own to begin on: the page semantics end their showpage
% banner without one, so a reading printed straight after it would be
% the tail of that line rather than a line the run can be read from
(\n) print
(PLACEMENTS ) print N =print (\n) print
(PAINTS ) print globaldict /paints get =print (\n) print
flush
quit
